BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Biomedical Data Science Seminar with José R. Zubizarreta\, PhD - 
 "The Experiment Inside the Model: Exact Weighting Representations for Spat
 ial\, Panel\, and Meta-Analytic Studies"
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T130000
DTSTAMP:20261104T183234Z
UID:geisel-6297-2026-11-12@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/calen
 dar/event_view.php?eid=6297&instance=2026-11-12\n\nDescription:\nThe Depar
 tment of Biomedical Data Science invites you to attend a seminar in person
  on Thursday\, November 12 from 12:00-1:00pm at DHMC\, Auditorium H with J
 osé R. Zubizarreta\, PhD\,  Professor of Biostatistics\, Harvard T.H. Cha
 n School of Public Health\, and the Daniel C. Tosteson Professor of Health
  Care Policy at Harvard Medical School.\nTalk title: “The Experiment Ins
 ide the Model: Exact Weighting Representations for Spatial\, Panel\, and M
 eta-Analytic Studies”\nLight refreshments will be provided on a first-co
 me\, first-served basis.\nThe presentation will take place both in person 
 at DHMC\, Auditorium H (and via Zoom) and we encourage as many of you to a
 ttend in person as possible. Please write to biomedical.data.science@dartm
 outh.edu to request the Zoom login details.\nPlease see description below 
 for more details.\nPlease invite your fellow faculty colleagues\, research
  associates\, graduate trainees\, and post-docs!\nPresentation Summary\nIn
  observational studies\, a fitted model may yield an estimate of a treatme
 nt effect without making explicit the comparison on which that estimate re
 sts. Which observations are compared\, and how much does each contribute? 
 Which features of a randomized experiment\, if any\, does the comparison r
 eproduce? These questions arise in spatial regressions with Gaussian proce
 ss errors\, two-way fixed effects analyses of staggered policy adoption\, 
 and mixed effects meta-analyses of trials. In this lecture\, we derive exa
 ct\, closed-form weighting representations of these estimators. In each ca
 se\, the weights solve a common optimization problem that minimizes their 
 dispersion subject to balance requirements. These finite-sample identities
  make explicit the comparisons implicit in the models\, providing a basis 
 for examining their causal interpretation and reconsidering their model sp
 ecification. (i) For spatial data\, specifying the error structure of a li
 near model is equivalent to including a set of hidden covariates under a p
 articular form of regularization. (ii) For panel data\, the weights reveal
  how two-way fixed effects and related estimators borrow information acros
 s units and time periods\, clarifying the assumptions about the dynamics o
 f treatment effects needed to justify that borrowing. (iii) For meta-analy
 tic data\, the weights identify studies and observations that are poorly a
 ligned with the covariate distribution of the target population\, helping 
 to assess when their exclusion is prudent. These representations give rise
  to diagnostics that open the analysis to scrutiny\, and they bring method
 s that appear unrelated onto common ground.\nBiography\nJosé R. Zubizarre
 ta\, PhD\, is the Daniel C. Tosteson Professor of Health Care Policy at Ha
 rvard Medical School\, Professor of Biostatistics at the Harvard T.H. Chan
  School of Public Health\, and a Faculty Affiliate in the Department of St
 atistics at Harvard University. His research focuses on developing statist
 ical methods for causal inference to advance health care and public policy
 . Dr. Zubizarreta earned his PhD in Statistics from the Wharton School of 
 the University of Pennsylvania\, an MA in Economics from Universidad de Ch
 ile\, and an undergraduate degree in Engineering from Pontificia Universid
 ad Católica de Chile. A former Fulbright Scholar\, Dr. Zubizarreta has re
 ceived research support from the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation\, the Warren A
 lpert Foundation\, and other major research organizations. He serves as an
  Associate Editor for several leading journals\, including the Annals of A
 pplied Statistics\, the Harvard Data Science Review\, and the Journal of t
 he American Statistical Association. Dr. Zubizarreta is an elected Fellow 
 of the American Statistical Association (ASA). His honors include the Kenn
 eth Rothman\, William Cochran\, and Tom Ten Have Memorial Awards\, as well
  as the ASA’s Mid-Career Excellence Award in Health Policy Statistics. D
 r. Zubizarreta has also received the Myrto Lefkopoulou Distinguished Lectu
 reship at Harvard and has been selected to deliver the Wayne A. Fuller Dis
 tinguished Lecture at Iowa State University.\n\nSponsor / organizer: Biome
 dical Data Science (  )\n\nSource: Geisel\n\nWhy this was included: explic
 it food-service wording\; food keyword near service context\n\nFood availa
 bility is inferred from the source listing\; verify details before attendi
 ng.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261104T183234Z
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
